# How to cite Basira. Checked by tests/test_packaging.py: `version` must equal
# basira.__version__. OWNER is the same placeholder README.md uses (docs/PUBLISHING.md).
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Basira in research or in a published investigation, please cite it."
title: "Basira: explainable forensics for edited and AI-generated images, audio and video"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Basira contributors"
version: "0.1.0"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/OWNER/basira"
url: "https://github.com/OWNER/basira"
abstract: >-
  Basira is a Python toolkit of documented signal-processing detectors for media forensics.
  It localises edits in images (splicing, copy-move, in-painting), audio (cuts, insertions)
  and video (removed, repeated and inserted frames), reads provenance metadata, and reports
  each detector's raw statistic, threshold and blind spots. It reports evidence, not proof:
  measured recall on public datasets is modest and is published with the code.
keywords:
  - media forensics
  - image forensics
  - audio forensics
  - video forensics
  - tamper detection
  - copy-move
  - splicing
